\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Compatible edge laws with no global extension}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{On the four-cycle #{0,1,2,3,0}, prescribe centered Gaussian edge
laws with unit variances and correlations
##{\rho_{01}=\rho_{12}=\rho_{23}=\frac9{10},
\qquad \rho_{30}=-\frac9{10}.}
Each edge covariance has eigenvalues #{1/10,19/10}, hence is positive
definite. All overlap singleton laws are #{N(0,1)}. Nevertheless
there is no global positive-semidefinite covariance matrix with these
entries, and therefore no global Gaussian extension.}

\proof{
\p{In any joint law with these second moments,
##{\|\phi_0-\phi_3\|_{L^2}=\sqrt{19/5},
\qquad
\|\phi_i-\phi_{i+1}\|_{L^2}=\sqrt{1/5}
\quad(i=0,1,2).}
But the first difference is the sum of the three consecutive
differences. The triangle inequality would give
#{\sqrt{19/5}\le3\sqrt{1/5}}, contradicting #{19>9}.
Any proposed positive-semidefinite covariance is a Gram matrix
of real vectors and obeys the same norm inequality. Thus the
obstruction already rules out semidefinite completion, without a
nondegeneracy assumption.}

\p{The angular description gives another view of the same
obstruction. Unit Gram vectors have consecutive angles
#{\alpha=\arccos(9/10)} and endpoint angle #{\pi-\alpha}.
For unit vectors with successive angles #{\beta,\gamma} and
#{\beta+\gamma\le\pi}, orthogonal decomposition along the middle
vector and Cauchy–Schwarz give
##{\langle u,w\rangle\ge
\cos\beta\cos\gamma-\sin\beta\sin\gamma
=\cos(\beta+\gamma).}
Since arccosine decreases, the endpoint angle is at most
#{\beta+\gamma}. If the sum exceeds #{\pi}, that bound is automatic.
Iteration gives endpoint angle at most #{3\alpha}. But
#{\alpha<\pi/4}, because #{9/10>1/\sqrt2}; hence
#{3\alpha<\pi-\alpha}, again a contradiction.}
}

\p{This example concerns the [presheaf of laws](fdip-0064) on the
edge cover: agreement on every overlap does not ensure existence.
It is a concrete [non-chordal](fdip-0068) obstruction of the kind distinguished by
\citet{Theorem 7, p. 120}{grone1984positive}. It does not say that
all data on a non-chordal graph fail to extend; the global four-cycle
law in \ref{fdip-006B} supplies data that do extend.}
